# s1-dl-wire (core half): the workspace service now answers `transfer/serverDownload/{begin,step,finish,abort,check}`
# (mixdog-workspace service.rs). It needs the links to the servers; the daemon wiring (CORE-WIRE-G9, with the ConnectionsService composition) gives them.

## Compose (CORE-WIRE-G9)
mixdog_workspace::service::WorkspaceService::set_remote_files(Arc<dyn mixdog_workspace::service::RemoteFileReaders>), once, after the workspace service
is built and the link pool exists. `RemoteFileReaders` has one method, `fn reader(&self, server_id: &str) -> Option<Arc<dyn RemoteFileReader>>`
(`None` answers "This SSH server is not connected. Connect it from the SSH server dialog."), which is exactly `LinkPool::reader`
(mixdog-connections remote_file_reader.rs). The adapter is one impl in the daemon:
    struct PoolFiles(Arc<LinkPool>);
    impl RemoteFileReaders for PoolFiles { fn reader(&self, id: &str) -> Option<Arc<dyn RemoteFileReader>> { self.0.reader(id) } }
    workspace.set_remote_files(Arc::new(PoolFiles(link_pool.clone())));
(or `impl RemoteFileReaders for LinkPool` in mixdog-connections, which then depends on mixdog-workspace for the trait: it already does, for RemoteFileReader.)
No Cargo edge is added by s1-dl-wire itself. Without the call the five methods are served and every begin answers not connected.

## SERVICE_PENDING (compose_flip.rs)
No name to remove: the five methods are new rows (pending-rows/s1-dl-wire.rows); they are served by the workspace service as soon as the rows exist.
The workspace service's `methods()` already lists them; the PROTO-WIRE rows and window_methods.json lines must land in the same step so the
daemon's served-methods-equal-rows check holds.

## Not done here
- A downloaded file stays listed for `check` for the life of the core (as the desktop's main process listed it for the life of the window); nothing
  removes `finished` entries, and a window closing does not cancel a core download (the desktop main process aborts the window's unfinished
  downloads from createServerDownloads.dispose; an abandoned one is discarded by the core after 120 s idle).
